"09_wincontrol.mst — real Win32 controls (DolphinDart DD10).

 `WinView` (06_winview.mst) answers Dolphin's LAYOUT protocol over a raw HWND.
 `WinControl` adds the two things a control needs beyond geometry: it CREATES
 itself, and it carries text.

 Created through the generated floor — `User32 createWindowEx:…`, straight from
 Dolphin's own pragma — not through the door's `mvpCreateButton`. That native
 was a DD7 spike convenience for proving WM_COMMAND arrived at all; a real
 control wants its own class name, style and id, and the prim already marshals
 every one of those (DD6b/c). The door keeps only the TOP-LEVEL window class,
 because that is the one whose WndProc has to be ours.

 SetWindowTextW/GetWindowTextW rather than WM_SETTEXT/WM_GETTEXT: same effect,
 and the generated wrappers already handle the string marshalling in both
 directions (`FFICoerce stringTemp:` out, a `Utf16Buffer` back)."
WinView subclass: WinControl [
    | controlId |

    "WS_CHILD | WS_VISIBLE — every control this class makes is a visible child
     of its parent. Styles beyond that are the subclass's business."
    WinControl class >> baseStyle [ ^16r50000000 ]

    "Create a real control of `aClassName` ('EDIT', 'BUTTON', 'STATIC', …) as a
     child of `aParent`, and answer it wrapped. The id is what comes back in
     WM_COMMAND's low word, which is how the owner tells its controls apart —
     so it is required, not optional."
    WinControl class >> class: aClassName parent: aParent id: anId
                        style: extraStyle text: aString [
        | h cls |
        "`lpClassName` is typed as a POINTER, not a string, and that is Win32's
         doing rather than the generator's: the parameter accepts either a
         name or a registered class ATOM, so the pragma cannot call it a
         string. The wrapper therefore marshals `lpWindowName` for us and
         leaves this one alone — passing a Smalltalk String here is refused
         outright ('FFI: cannot pass String as a pointer argument'), which is
         the floor doing its job. Marshal it here, free it under ensure:."
        cls := Utf16Buffer fromString: aClassName.
        h := [ User32
            createWindowEx: 0
            lpClassName: cls address
            lpWindowName: aString
            dwStyle: (self baseStyle bitOr: extraStyle)
            x: 0 y: 0 nWidth: 10 nHeight: 10
            hWndParent: aParent handle
            hMenu: anId
            hInstance: 0
            lpParam: 0 ] ensure: [ cls free ].
        "`isNil`, not `= 0`. CreateWindowExW returns a HANDLE, and the floor's
         `#h` convention answers nil for NULL (DD10) — so a `= 0` test here
         would answer false on failure and hand back a nil handle as if the
         call had worked. Every failure check on a handle return has to move
         with the convention."
        h isNil ifTrue: [
            ^Win32Error new setCode: Win32 lastError; signalWithCode ].
        ^(self onHandle: h parent: aParent) setControlId: anId
    ]

    setControlId: anId [ controlId := anId ]
    controlId [ ^controlId ]

    "── text ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     `text` reads what the user actually typed, which is the whole point of an
     edit control: the answer must come from the WINDOW, never from a cached
     copy this object set earlier."
    text [
        | n buf |
        n := User32 getWindowTextLength: hwnd.
        n = 0 ifTrue: [ ^'' ].
        buf := Utf16Buffer new: (n + 1) * 2.
        User32 getWindowText: hwnd lpString: buf address nMaxCount: n + 1.
        ^buf stringValue
    ]
    text: aString [
        User32 setWindowText: hwnd lpString: aString displayString.
        ^aString
    ]
]

"An EDIT control. ES_AUTOHSCROLL (16r80) keeps typing past the right edge from
 being swallowed, and WS_BORDER (16r800000) makes it look like a field."
WinControl subclass: WinTextEdit [
    WinTextEdit class >> in: aParent id: anId [
        ^self class: 'EDIT' parent: aParent id: anId
             style: 16r800080 text: ''
    ]
]

"A BUTTON. BS_PUSHBUTTON is 0, so the base style is the whole of it."
WinControl subclass: WinButton [
    WinButton class >> in: aParent id: anId text: aString [
        ^self class: 'BUTTON' parent: aParent id: anId style: 0 text: aString
    ]
]

"A STATIC label."
WinControl subclass: WinLabel [
    WinLabel class >> in: aParent id: anId text: aString [
        ^self class: 'STATIC' parent: aParent id: anId style: 0 text: aString
    ]
]
